<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">European Communities</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1951">1951</a>–<a href="/years/2009">2009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as European Community</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The European Communities were three organisations founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Luxembourg and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>: the European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">Steel</a> Community of 1951, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> and Euratom of 1957. They shared institutions from 1967, expanded from 1973 and became the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> created in 1993.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/european-communities.jpg" alt="European Communities" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sigsjak · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pillar_structure_of_the_European_Union.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greece entered the republic on the record growth of 1950 to 1973, and membership of the <b>European Communities</b> from 1981 brought sustained growth through industrial investment, infrastructure, European funds, tourism, shipping and a fast-growing service sector.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hellenic-republic">Hellenic Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1945 Belgium became a builder of the new Europe: Paul-Henri Spaak was a leading architect of the Treaty of Rome, Belgium co-founded Benelux, NATO and the <b>European Communities</b>, and Brussels became the main seat of the European Commission and the Council and, from 1967, of NATO’s headquarters.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the German occupation of 9 April 1940 to May 1945 Denmark became a founding member of NATO in 1949 and joined the <b>European Community</b> in 1973.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>After the German occupation of 1940–1945 Norway rebuilt itself as a social democratic welfare state; its voters rejected membership of the <b>European Community</b> in 1972 and 1994, and it is today a constitutional monarchy under Harald V, king since 1991.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Freed from Soviet constraints, Finland applied to the <b>European Communities</b> and, after a referendum in 1994, joined the European Union on 1 January 1995; it adopted the euro in 1999, unlike its Nordic neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The German political scientist Fritz Scharpf described a “joint-decision trap” in Germany and the <b>European Community</b>, where the need for agreement between levels of government blocked reform.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
